            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I think this is specifically an issue with our internal C return code type. Cython uses it in cases where the internal C return value is a C integer denoting success (0) or exception (-1), but where the result in regular Python is represented as a `None` value. That type is declared as a C integer type, but it coerces to a Python `None`, after exception propagation.\n\nThe node tree that we construct takes the return code value, coerces it to a Python `None`, but then coerces the result to a boolean value, looking at the integer return type rather than Python `None`. The (early) change in 3.3 that probably broke this was to make Cython aware of C integers coercing to Python `int` (rather than just `object`). I hadn't considered the special return type at the time.\n\nThere are two things to do here. One is to make sure we infer `object` for coerced C return types. The second is then to notice that the boolean result of `None` is constant false, and not apply any coercion at all. I just tested a similar situation with `f\"{a_set.add(1)}\"`, and that already generates efficient code (with a constant `\"None\"` string result), so there is good precedent.",
